<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/hero-home-after-rain.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/appraiser-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>Flat Roof &amp; Stucco Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Flat Roofs, Parapets and Stucco: The New Mexico Way</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Pueblo Revival and Territorial homes hide their roofs behind parapets, and that design changes how damage is found and priced. A New Mexico flat roof appraisal accounts for the membrane, the drainage and the stucco that wraps it all.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s;margin-top:26px"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn light" href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Styles</small><b>Pueblo Revival, Territorial</b></div><div><small>Systems</small><b>BUR, mod-bit, TPO, foam</b></div><div><small>Key dispute</small><b>Stucco color and texture</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How these roofs work</h2>
<p>A flat roof in New Mexico is rarely perfectly flat. It slopes gently toward canales or scuppers set through the parapet wall, often with crickets to steer water away from corners. The roofing membrane turns up the inside of the parapet and is capped with stucco, metal coping or a membrane wrap. When any part of that assembly fails, water reaches the walls and ceilings below.</p>
<h2>Roofing systems on flat New Mexico homes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Built-up roofing (BUR)</strong> with gravel or a reflective coating, common on older homes.</li>
<li><strong>Modified bitumen</strong>, torch-down or self-adhered, with granulated cap sheets.</li>
<li><strong>TPO and other single-ply membranes</strong>, increasingly used on newer construction.</li>
<li><strong>Spray polyurethane foam with elastomeric coating</strong>, popular for its insulation value.</li>
<li><strong>Elastomeric roof coatings</strong> applied over existing roofs as a maintenance layer.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each system responds to hail and wind differently. Hail can bruise modified bitumen, fracture foam beneath its coating, or puncture an aging membrane. Wind can lift edges at parapets and pull coping loose.</p>
<h2>Ponding and drainage</h2>
<p>Water that stands on a roof for more than a couple of days after rain is a sign of drainage problems. Carriers often point to ponding as evidence of long-term deterioration rather than storm damage. Sometimes that’s fair; sometimes a storm clogged drains with debris or damaged a cricket. We document drainage carefully because causation affects coverage, while the panel’s job is to value the damage it finds.</p>
<h3>Repairability</h3>
<p>Not every flat roof can be patched. Brittle older membranes, multiple layers, saturated insulation, and manufacturer restrictions on repairs all affect whether a repair is realistic. Core samples can show what lies beneath the surface and are often worth the cost on a disputed claim.</p>
<h2>Stucco damage and matching</h2>
<p>Stucco is the skin of most New Mexico homes. Hail impacts chip and crack it, wind-driven debris gouges it, and water from roof leaks discolors it. Repairs raise three questions:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>System type.</strong> Traditional three-coat cement stucco, one-coat systems, and synthetic (acrylic) finishes require different repair methods.</li>
<li><strong>Color and texture.</strong> Patched stucco seldom matches weathered stucco around it. Integral-color finishes fade, and hand-applied textures vary by crew.</li>
<li><strong>Extent of refinishing.</strong> Whether a patch, a full elevation, or the entire house must be refinished for a reasonably uniform appearance.</li>
</ol>
<p>The policy’s language on matching and the physical facts both matter. The panel can value what a uniform repair costs; whether the policy requires it may be a coverage question.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Check the roof area</b>Flat roofs are measured in squares like pitched roofs, but parapet wall height and wrap-up add material. Our <a href="/claim-estimators/roof-area-pitch-converter/">roof area and pitch converter</a> helps check the carrier’s quantities.</div>
<h2>Interior damage from flat-roof leaks</h2>
<p>Leaks around parapets often show up as stains on vigas and latillas, cracked plaster, and wet insulation in the shallow cavity above the ceiling. Those repairs can involve specialty trades. See <a href="/appraiser-services/water-damage-appraisal/">water damage appraisal</a> for interior losses and <a href="/appraiser-services/hail-roof-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a> for pitched roofs.</p>
<h3>What a New Mexico flat roof appraisal adds</h3>
<p>Generic estimating software treats a flat roof as a simple rectangle. A New Mexico flat roof appraisal counts the parapet wrap, the canales, the coping and the crickets, and it asks whether the stucco cap on each wall was damaged by the same storm. Those details are often where the money is.</p>
<h2>Where these homes are common</h2>
<p>Flat-roofed homes are found across <a href="/cities-we-serve/santa-fe/">Santa Fe</a>, <a href="/cities-we-serve/albuquerque/">Albuquerque</a>, <a href="/cities-we-serve/las-cruces/">Las Cruces</a> and <a href="/cities-we-serve/alamogordo/">Alamogordo</a>. If your carrier has priced a patch when the system is failing, a <a href="/appraiser-services/claim-estimate-review/">claim estimate review</a> is a good first step.</p>

<h2>Questions about flat roof &amp; stucco appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is ponding water on my flat roof a covered loss?</summary><div class="a"><p>Ponding itself is often treated as a maintenance issue, but storm damage that causes ponding or leaks may be covered. Your policy wording controls coverage.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will my insurer pay to re-stucco the whole house?</summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the policy and how visible the mismatch would be. Appraisal can price a uniform repair, but matching coverage disputes may need legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can a flat roof just be recoated after hail?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sometimes, if the underlying membrane and insulation are sound. Core samples and a careful inspection help decide whether a coating is a real repair.</p></div></details></div>
